No, raising the pool size would only delay the problem. This looks like a connection leak.

In `db.js`, `withClient` releases the client only if `fn` resolves:

```js
const client = await pool.connect();
const result = await fn(client);
client.release();
```

If `fn` throws, for example on a query error, `client.release()` never runs and that connection is lost to the pool for good. `pool.log` matches that pattern. All 20 connections stay checked out (`idle=0`), and the waiting queue keeps growing from 7 to 28 without ever draining. The pool isn't too small for the load; its connections are not being returned. A pool of 40 or 100 would fill up the same way, just more slowly, and it would also put more load on Postgres.

The log also covers only about 2.5 minutes (14:02:11 to 14:04:42), not an hour. It shows the pool already full, but not how it got there.

Probably not. The pool looks like it is leaking connections, not undersized.

In `db.js:11-16`, `withClient` only releases the client when `fn` succeeds:

```js
const client = await pool.connect();
const result = await fn(client);
client.release();
```

If `fn` throws, for example on a failed query, `client.release()` never runs and that connection stays checked out for good. Each error permanently takes one of the 20 slots.

The log fits that pattern. `idle` stays at 0 and `waiting` climbs steadily from 7 to 28 without ever draining. A pool that was only too small would show idle connections now and then as queries finished. With a bigger `max`, the leaked connections would just take longer to fill it.

The log only covers about 2.5 minutes (14:02:11 to 14:04:42), not an hour, so I can't rule out a burst of real load. To confirm the leak, check whether callers of `withClient` were throwing errors while the pool was saturated.
